\section{What the models actually do}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Climatology} always predicts the median training value.
\item \textbf{Persistence} copies the most recent observed \cn{} value. It is usually
hard to beat over a few minutes because the atmosphere changes continuously.
\item \textbf{Recent mean} averages the six observed \cn{} values.
\item \textbf{LightGBM} is a strong conventional tree ensemble using the flattened,
identical history and weather inputs. Its fixed configuration uses 300 trees,
learning rate 0.03, 31 leaves, maximum depth 6, \(L_2\) regularization 1.0,
all rows and features per tree, deterministic seed 17, and no search.
\item \textbf{Compact MLP} is a four-block, width-192 neural network. Unlike the first
experiment, it consumes the complete six-row context.
\item \textbf{Strata-OT Surface} projects each time step to width 192, uses six
Transformer blocks, a four-expert regime router, temporal pooling, and a residual
around honest per-example persistence.
\end{itemize}

Both neural models predict a Gaussian location and positive scale plus ordered
quantiles. The scale uses a smooth softplus parameterization. A single multiplicative
scale factor is fitted only on the validation calibration block and then frozen.
Location predictions are not recalibrated.

